phishing_docs = [
("NIST SP 800-177 Rev1 §2.1 - Email Auth", "Email authentication protocols form the technical backbone of phishing prevention infrastructure. SPF (Sender Policy Framework) operates at the DNS level, allowing domain owners to publish a list of authorised mail servers. When a receiving mail server gets an email claiming to be from a domain, it queries DNS for the SPF record and checks whether the sending server's IP is listed. A hard fail policy (-all) instructs receiving servers to reject emails from unauthorised sources. DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail) adds a cryptographic signature to email messages using a private key held by the sending domain. The corresponding public key is published in DNS. Receiving servers verify the signature to confirm the email was sent by the legitimate domain and has not been modified in transit. DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication Reporting and Conformance) ties SPF and DKIM together by specifying what action receivers should take when authentication fails: none (monitor only), quarantine (deliver to spam), or reject (block delivery). DMARC also enables aggregate reporting (rua) and forensic reporting (ruf), giving domain owners visibility into authentication failures and potential abuse. Organisations must implement all three protocols together — SPF and DKIM alone do not protect the RFC5322.From header visible to users. Only DMARC aligns authentication results with the visible sender address. All domains including parked and inactive domains must have DMARC records to prevent attackers from exploiting otherwise unprotected domains for spoofing campaigns."),

("NIST SP 800-177 Rev1 §3 - Anti-Phishing Gateways", "Secure email gateways provide multi-layered analysis of inbound and outbound email traffic to detect and block phishing attempts. Reputation-based filtering is the first layer — the gateway checks sender IP addresses, domains, and URLs against real-time threat intelligence feeds maintained by security vendors. Newly registered domains (NRDs) — domains registered within the past 30 days — are disproportionately used for phishing and should be treated with heightened suspicion. Content analysis examines email headers, body text, and embedded links for phishing indicators including urgency language, grammatical errors, mismatched URLs where display text differs from the actual link destination, and requests for sensitive information. Sandboxed attachment detonation executes email attachments in an isolated virtual machine to observe their behaviour — malicious attachments that evade signature detection may be caught by behavioural analysis. URL rewriting routes all links through a proxy that performs time-of-click analysis — even if a URL was clean at delivery time, it is checked again when the user clicks, catching delayed detonation attacks where malicious content is activated after gateway scanning. Machine learning models trained on millions of phishing and legitimate emails provide detection capability against novel phishing variants that evade signature rules. Outbound scanning detects compromised accounts sending phishing from within the organisation and prevents the organisation from becoming a spam source."),

("CISA Anti-Phishing Guide 2023 §4 - MFA", "Multi-factor authentication is the single most impactful control for reducing the effectiveness of credential phishing attacks. When credentials are stolen through phishing, MFA prevents attackers from using those credentials to access accounts and systems. However, not all MFA implementations provide equal protection. SMS one-time passwords transmit codes over the cellular network and are vulnerable to SIM-swapping attacks — attackers convince or bribe mobile carrier employees to transfer the victim's phone number to an attacker-controlled SIM card, enabling interception of SMS OTP codes. Time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) generated by authenticator apps are vulnerable to real-time phishing attacks using adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) proxies — the victim enters their TOTP code into a phishing page, which the attacker immediately forwards to the legitimate site before the code expires. FIDO2 hardware security keys and platform passkeys provide phishing-resistant authentication because authentication is bound to the specific website origin — a credential registered at example.com cannot authenticate to a spoofed site at examp1e.com, regardless of how convincing the phishing page appears. Organisations should prioritise deployment of phishing-resistant MFA for administrative accounts, email accounts, VPN endpoints, and cloud management consoles. Conditional access policies should require step-up authentication for high-risk actions such as password changes, MFA enrollment changes, and access from new devices or locations."),

("CISA Advisory AA22-074A - Business Email Compromise", "Business Email Compromise represents one of the highest-impact phishing variants, resulting in billions of dollars in losses annually. Unlike mass phishing campaigns, BEC attacks are carefully researched and targeted. Attackers conduct open-source intelligence gathering on target organisations to understand their structure, key personnel, vendor relationships, and ongoing business activities. Common BEC attack scenarios exploit knowledge of legitimate business processes: vendor payment fraud involves impersonating a known vendor to redirect invoice payments to attacker-controlled bank accounts; CEO fraud involves impersonating the CEO or CFO to instruct finance personnel to process urgent wire transfers; payroll diversion involves impersonating an employee to HR or payroll departments to redirect salary payments; and real estate wire fraud intercepts communications between buyers, sellers, and escrow companies to redirect property transaction funds. Technical indicators of BEC attempts include slight misspellings in display names (CEO Nam vs CEO Name), lookalike domains registered by attackers, legitimate-appearing email signatures, and messages that create urgency and request secrecy. Defensive controls include: DMARC enforcement in reject mode; email banners identifying external senders; out-of-band verification for all payment changes above a defined threshold; callback procedures using previously established contact numbers; and dual-authorisation requirements for wire transfers. Finance and HR staff require role-specific BEC awareness training updated to reflect current attack techniques."),

("OWASP Phishing Prevention CSC §3 - Web Defences", "Web application security controls provide critical defences against phishing attacks that target users through compromised or spoofed web pages. HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) forces browsers to connect only over HTTPS, preventing SSL stripping attacks that could expose user credentials. The HSTS preload list hardcodes major websites as HTTPS-only in browsers, providing protection even for first visits before an HSTS header has been received. Content Security Policy headers restrict the sources from which scripts, stylesheets, and other resources can be loaded, preventing attackers from injecting malicious scripts into legitimate pages through XSS vulnerabilities. Subresource Integrity attributes on script and link tags cryptographically verify that third-party resources have not been tampered with — if an attacker compromises a CDN or third-party JavaScript library, SRI prevents the malicious code from executing on pages that enforce it. Certificate Transparency logs record all TLS certificates issued by certificate authorities. Monitoring CT logs enables organisations to detect certificates issued for their domain names or convincing lookalike domains, providing early warning of phishing infrastructure. Organisations should register common typosquats and homograph variants of their primary domain, and should implement a process for monitoring domain registration services for new registrations that closely match their brand names."),

("Microsoft Security BEC Playbook 2024", "When a phishing or BEC incident is detected, the incident response timeline is critical because attackers can cause significant damage quickly after initial access. Within the first 30 minutes, the following actions must be completed: revoke all active sessions for the compromised account using the identity provider's session management interface; force a password reset and require re-enrollment of MFA factors; check email account settings for attacker-created forwarding rules, inbox filters, or auto-reply configurations designed to maintain access and hide attack-related emails from the legitimate user; review sent items and drafts for phishing emails sent to the victim's contacts; check for OAuth application authorisations the attacker may have granted to maintain persistent access after password reset. Within the first two hours: examine authentication logs for lateral movement — has the attacker used the compromised account to access other systems, applications, or service accounts; identify all emails sent by the attacker from the compromised account and assess whether recipients took any action; determine whether any financial transactions, document access, or data transfers occurred. Within 24 hours: notify all recipients of attacker-sent phishing emails; review access logs for systems accessed using the compromised account; complete forensic preservation of relevant logs and artefacts; and submit the phishing email and IOCs to threat intelligence sharing platforms."),

("SANS Security Awareness Research 2024 §2", "Effective security awareness training programmes must move beyond compliance-checkbox annual training to create measurable, lasting changes in security behaviour. Research consistently shows that annual training has minimal lasting impact — knowledge and vigilance decay within weeks of training. Modern behaviour-based security awareness programmes apply principles from behavioural psychology and adult learning theory. Spaced repetition — delivering short, focused learning modules at regular intervals rather than one long annual session — leverages the testing effect and spacing effect to produce durable knowledge retention. Just-in-time training delivered immediately after a security behaviour failure — such as clicking a simulated phishing link — is more effective than pre-scheduled training because the learner is motivated by the recent failure and the lesson is directly relevant to their experience. Positive reinforcement of correct security behaviours, such as recognising and reporting phishing attempts, is more effective than shame-based or punitive approaches to security awareness. Gamification elements including leaderboards, badges, and competition between teams increase engagement with training content. Metrics that indicate programme effectiveness include: phishing click rates and trends over time; credential submission rates on phishing simulations; and especially reporting rates — the proportion of employees who report suspicious emails — because a strong reporting culture provides a real-time early warning system for phishing campaigns."),

("CISA AiTM Phishing Alert 2024", "Adversary-in-the-middle phishing represents a significant evolution beyond traditional credential harvesting. AiTM frameworks such as Evilginx, Modlishka, and Muraena act as transparent proxies between the victim and the legitimate target website. The victim believes they are interacting directly with the legitimate site — they see the correct URL in the browser address bar (via a convincing lookalike domain), the legitimate site's actual content, and they receive a valid TLS certificate. The proxy intercepts the full authentication exchange including the completed MFA challenge and the resulting authenticated session cookie. The attacker can then use the captured session cookie to access the account without credentials or MFA, bypassing traditional MFA protections. Detection of AiTM attacks relies on identity platform signals including: impossible travel alerts (login from two geographically distant locations within a short time); token replay detection (the same session token used from multiple IP addresses); anomalous user agent strings; and behavioural baselines for login patterns. Response requires session revocation rather than simply password reset, because the attacker's access is based on the session token rather than credentials. FIDO2 passkeys and hardware security keys defeat AiTM attacks because the cryptographic response is computed for the specific domain of the legitimate site — the passkey registered at example.com computes a different response for example.com than for the AiTM lookalike domain."),

("ISO 27002:2022 §8.23 - Web Filtering", "Web filtering controls prevent users from accessing known malicious websites including phishing pages, malware distribution sites, and command and control infrastructure. DNS-based filtering operates at the network layer by blocking DNS resolution of malicious domains — when a user's browser or application attempts to resolve a malicious domain, the DNS resolver returns a blocked response rather than the actual IP address. DNS filtering is effective because it is transparent to applications and cannot be bypassed by individual users without changing DNS settings. Protective DNS services offered by CISA (and commercial providers) maintain comprehensive blocklists updated in near-real-time from threat intelligence feeds. Category-based filtering blocks access to newly registered domains (NRDs), which are disproportionately associated with phishing and malware, regardless of whether specific domains appear in threat feeds yet. Browser isolation technologies render web content in a remote, cloud-based browser and stream only the visual output to the user — the user's local browser receives a pixel stream rather than executable web content, preventing drive-by downloads and credential harvesting even if the user visits a malicious page. Remote browser isolation is particularly valuable for high-risk user populations such as executives, finance staff, and privileged IT administrators."),

("ACSC Phishing Incident Response 2023", "Phishing incident response procedures must be documented, tested, and rehearsed before an incident occurs. The incident response plan for phishing should address the following scenarios: single user credential compromise via phishing; mass phishing campaign targeting the organisation's users; supply chain phishing through a compromised vendor or partner; BEC financial fraud attempt; and VIP targeting of executives or board members. For each scenario, the plan should specify: initial triage steps to confirm the incident type and scope; immediate containment actions; evidence preservation requirements; internal notification and escalation procedures; external notification requirements including law enforcement, regulators, and affected third parties; communication templates for notifying affected users; and recovery steps. The plan should include a contact directory with 24/7 contacts for: the incident response team; executive leadership; legal counsel; cyber insurance carrier; external incident response retainer; law enforcement contacts at FBI and CISA; and the organisation's banking relationships for BEC scenarios requiring rapid recall of wire transfers. Phishing response tabletop exercises should be conducted at least annually, simulating a realistic scenario that tests decision-making, communication, and coordination across all relevant functions including security, IT, finance, HR, legal, and communications."),
]

