vuln_docs = [
("NIST SP 800-40r4 §3 - VM Lifecycle", "The vulnerability management lifecycle is a continuous, iterative process that must be embedded in the organisation's operational rhythm rather than treated as a periodic project. The six-phase lifecycle begins with asset discovery — maintaining an accurate, comprehensive inventory of all technology assets is the foundation because vulnerabilities cannot be managed in unknown assets. Asset discovery must encompass all asset categories: physical servers and workstations, virtual machines and containers, network infrastructure devices, cloud instances and services, operational technology and industrial control systems, mobile devices, and shadow IT assets deployed without formal IT approval. Authenticated vulnerability scanning — scanning with credentials that allow the scanner to log in to assessed systems — produces significantly more complete and accurate results than unauthenticated scanning because it can access installed software version information, security configuration settings, patch status, and other attributes not visible to external scanners. Scan coverage must be comprehensive: all internet-facing systems should be scanned at minimum weekly; internal systems monthly; cloud environments should use continuous configuration assessment tools. Scan results must be normalised to the National Vulnerability Database CVE identifiers and CVSS scores to enable consistent tracking and comparison over time. Vulnerability data must be correlated with asset inventory to produce asset-centric vulnerability views that show the complete vulnerability profile of each critical asset. Prioritisation frameworks combining CVSS base score, environmental score adjusted for asset criticality and exposure, EPSS probability of exploitation, and KEV catalogue membership produce more actionable priority rankings than CVSS alone."),

("CISA KEV BOD 22-01 - Prioritisation", "CISA Binding Operational Directive 22-01 established the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue as the authoritative source for vulnerabilities confirmed to be actively exploited by threat actors. The directive requires federal civilian executive branch agencies to remediate KEV entries within 14 days of catalogue addition. Private sector organisations should treat KEV membership as a critical prioritisation signal independent of CVSS score. The KEV catalogue demonstrates a fundamental limitation of CVSS-only prioritisation: many high-CVSS vulnerabilities are never significantly exploited in practice, while many moderate-CVSS vulnerabilities are actively and widely exploited because they are easy to exploit, widely deployed, or effective in attack chains. A vulnerability with CVSS 6.5 that appears in the KEV catalogue represents a higher immediate risk than a theoretical CVSS 9.8 vulnerability that has no known exploitation. The Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) complements KEV by providing machine learning-based probability estimates for exploitation within the next 30 days. EPSS considers factors including vulnerability characteristics, exploitation history of similar vulnerabilities, threat actor interest, and availability of public proof-of-concept exploit code. Organisations should implement a tiered remediation SLA framework: KEV and EPSS high-probability vulnerabilities on critical assets should be remediated within 15 days; CVSS critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing assets within 15 days; CVSS high vulnerabilities within 30 days; CVSS medium within 90 days; and CVSS low within 180 days or as part of scheduled maintenance windows."),
]

