cloud_docs = [
("CIS AWS Benchmarks v2.0 §2 - IAM", "AWS Identity and Access Management security hardening is foundational to secure cloud operations. The root account — created automatically when an AWS account is opened — has unrestricted access to all AWS services and billing. The root account must be protected with hardware MFA, its access keys must be deleted, and it must never be used for day-to-day operations. IAM best practices for AWS: create individual IAM users rather than sharing credentials; assign permissions using IAM roles and groups rather than directly to users; apply the principle of least privilege — grant only the permissions required for each role; use IAM Access Analyzer to identify resource policies granting unintended external access; enable AWS CloudTrail in all regions with log file integrity validation to detect credential misuse; configure AWS Config to continuously monitor IAM configurations against defined rules; use AWS IAM Identity Center (formerly SSO) for federated access using corporate identity providers to centralise access management; implement permission boundaries to limit maximum permissions for IAM entities; and use service control policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations to establish guardrails preventing privileged actions across all accounts in the organisation. EC2 instances and Lambda functions requiring AWS API access should use IAM instance profiles and execution roles respectively rather than embedding access keys in code or configuration files. Long-lived IAM user access keys should be identified using IAM credential reports and rotated or eliminated. IAM access keys older than 90 days without recent use should be disabled and investigated."),

("NIST SP 800-204C §4 - Zero Trust Microservices", "Zero trust architecture for cloud-native microservices eliminates the implicit trust in internal network communications that characterised traditional perimeter-based security models. In microservices architectures, applications consist of dozens to hundreds of independent services communicating over a network — the internal network between services was previously treated as trusted. Zero trust applies authentication and authorisation to every service-to-service API call regardless of network location. Service mesh technologies implement zero trust networking at the infrastructure layer without requiring application code changes. Istio and Linkerd use the SPIFFE (Secure Production Identity Framework For Everyone) standard to issue cryptographic identities to workloads, enabling mutual TLS authentication between services. mTLS ensures that both parties in a service-to-service communication are authenticated and that communications are encrypted. Authorization policies define which services are permitted to call which API endpoints using which HTTP methods — a compromised service cannot make arbitrary API calls to other services. Workload identity federation enables cloud services to authenticate to external APIs using short-lived, automatically rotated tokens rather than long-lived service account keys stored in configuration files. API gateway security controls — authentication, rate limiting, input validation, and threat detection — should be applied at the ingress layer for all external API traffic. Zero trust network policies should be continuously monitored and enforced using runtime security tools that detect and alert on policy violations."),
]

